<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is a state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the Baltic coast, with Schwerin as its capital and Rostock as its largest city. It unites the historic lands of Mecklenburg with the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> left in Germany after 1945, including the island of Rügen. Created in 1945, broken up into districts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> in 1952 and re-established in 1990, it is the most thinly populated German state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern.jpg" alt="Mecklenburg-Vorpommern" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mecklenburg-Western_Pomerania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The state covers 23,300 km2, the sixth largest German state by area. Its Baltic coastline of about 2,000 km has peninsulas, lagoons and islands, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s two biggest, Rügen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a>. In the south lies the Mecklenburg Lake District, where Lake Müritz is the largest lake in Germany. The name Vorpommern comes from West Slavic po more, land at the sea, with the German prefix vor- meaning the nearer part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement began after the Ice Age, about 10,000 BCE. Germanic peoples recorded there about two thousand years ago mostly left during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>, and from the sixth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a> settled the area: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> in Mecklenburg, the Veleti and Rani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Vikings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> set up trading posts along the coast, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reric">Reric</a>, Ralswiek and Menzlin. In the late 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, conquered the Obotrites and Christianized them, and German settlers followed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>. Mecklenburg became a duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1348 and its two grand duchies of Mecklenburg-Schwerin and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> were created in 1815.</p><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1121 to 1138, and its dukes were later vassals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> before staying with the Empire from 1227. Western Pomerania was under Swedish rule from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 until 1815 and then became part of the Prussian Province of Pomerania, remaining so until 1945.</p><p>The Soviet military administration created the Province of Mecklenburg and West Pomerania on 9 July 1945, by order of Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Georgy Zhukov</a>. Expellees from the former eastern territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> raised the population by 40%, and in 1949 they made up 43.3% of it. The state's name was shortened to Land Mecklenburg in 1947, and it became a constituent state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> in 1949. In 1952 it was divided into the districts of Rostock, Schwerin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg">Neubrandenburg</a>. At reunification in 1990 the state was re-established along its postwar boundaries under the name Mecklenburg-Vorpommern.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Schwerin is the state capital, and Rostock, with about 205,000 inhabitants, is the largest city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock">University of Rostock</a>, founded in 1419, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-greifswald">University of Greifswald</a>, founded in 1456, are among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The old Hanseatic towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, and red Brick Gothic churches and houses are a feature of many towns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mecklenburg-Vorpommern” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Schwerin became the capital of the Mecklenburg dukes and later of the Grand Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, and is today the capital of <b>Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-schwerin">County of Schwerin</a></li><li><blockquote>Known as <b>Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</b> until 1947, the Land became part of the German Democratic Republic in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg">Free State of Mecklenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1701 it belonged to the Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz and is now part of <b>Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg">Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now part of <b>Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-schwerin">Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin</a></li><li><blockquote>Rügen stayed Pomeranian, then Swedish and Prussian, and is now part of <b>Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li></ul></section>
